From 517f6a864baad41d35c0b00fedd1fec8e75a3a62 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Hades <15710513+hadesdevls@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Thu, 12 Nov 2026 04:46:37 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] system: check this setup
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

System > Check this setup plays short clips that the build of LibreELEC
Hades made (/usr/share/hades/check, the package hades-check: a test card
in SDR, HDR10, HLG and Dolby Vision 8.1 and 5, a soft chime in each sound
format that can be made well, and a walk round the speakers in PCM), and
says in plain words what went out and how. Where the answer depends on
what the user sees or hears, it asks.

- The window closes for the check, which runs in a thread of the service,
  and opens again at the end: it is a dialog over everything, and a test
  picture plays over the whole screen. While a clip plays, the property
  hades.check.running of the Home window makes Kodi publish what its
  player information would show (the mode, the HDR signal the driver
  holds, native Dolby Vision and why not, the sound output, the sentence
  of the overview page); the property is taken away when the clip ends,
  whatever happens. The sentence of each picture uses Estuary's words of
  that page (loaded in every skin), so both say the same.
- A picture: one question with a description of what should have been
  seen ("It looked right", "It looked wrong", "Show it again"); "wrong"
  asks what was seen, and each answer says what to do. SDR asks too how
  many of the four dark and of the four light squares could be seen
  (black level and clipping), with the name of Kodi's own setting where
  it is the answer. Nothing of the picture settings is changed.
- The speakers: the walk that fits "Number of channels" plays, and the
  screen names the speaker that should sound; where the display takes
  fewer channels of PCM (seen twice, after the first speaker), the walk
  of those channels plays instead, and the result says so.
- The sound formats (passthrough): Kodi does not trust the audio data of
  the display for passthrough, and neither does the check; that data only
  decides which sound plays first. Each chime plays in passthrough; the
  question "Did you hear the chime?" comes only when the output was seen
  to send that bitstream while the chime sounded. Only what is heard is
  switched on; "no" or a closed question switches nothing on and nothing
  off; a format that is on and is not heard is offered, in a question of
  its own that keeps it on by default, to be switched off. Where
  passthrough was off, it is switched on for a format that was heard, and
  the formats that were not heard are set off, so that exactly what was
  heard goes out. Formats whose sound cannot be made well (DTS, DTS-HD)
  are not played: the result says how to set them by hand.
- What the settings were is written to /storage/.cache/hades-check/
  restore.json before the first change; at the end, what they become is
  written there, set, read back, and the file removed. If Kodi stops in
  between, hades-check-restore (hades-check-restore.service, before Kodi
  starts) puts the file back into guisettings.xml.
- The results are said after each check and together at the end; "Show
  the last result" says them again in the language of the moment, and
  summary.txt beside the journal holds them in English for a diagnostic
  report. Each result goes into kodi.log too ("check: ...").

Nothing of it runs or plays unless the user starts it. The category is
not shown on a system without the clips.

The decisions are in resources/lib/hades_check.py, without Kodi, for the
host tests; the part that asks, plays and shows is hades_check_ui.py.

Texts in English and the three Spanish ones (tú in Spain and Mexico, vos
in Argentina; "vídeo" in Spain, "video" in Argentina and Mexico;
"configuración" and "televisión" in Mexico), numbered from 33640 so that
they meet none of the other changes of this version.
